        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>That's a modest week: two posts a working day, spread across four networks. It isn't a campaign launch. Nothing here includes paid media, which every route bills separately. Every figure on this page was read at its source on October 14, 2026.</p>

  <h2>What does an in-house week cost?</h2>

  <p>In-house, you don't buy ten posts. You buy a person's week, and the week costs the same whether it produces ten posts or four.</p>

  <p>The closest occupation in US wage statistics is the marketing specialist. Designers and video editors sit in their own series.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">At the May 2025 national median, a US marketing specialist earned $78,760 a year, or $37.87 an hour; the 10th percentile earned $43,390 and the 90th $155,480.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: US Bureau of Labor Statistics, Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics, May 2025, a national survey of employers that excludes the self-employed.</p>

  <p>Salary is not the cost. An employer also pays for benefits, payroll taxes and paid leave, and the federal series that measures them puts the total at about 1.43 times the wage.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Private employers paid $46.89 an hour worked in June 2026, of which $32.82 was wages and salaries, so total compensation ran about 1.43 times the wage.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: US Bureau of Labor Statistics, Employer Costs for Employee Compensation, June 2026, released September 2026, a national establishment survey.</p>

  <p>The method is two steps: the annual median times 1.43, divided by 52 weeks.</p>

