    </table>
  </div>

  <p>One specialist can make the defined week alone if the visuals stay simple. Add a designer for the carousels and the video edits and the week is about $3,894, before a single tool or stock license.</p>

  <p>Europe runs on the same arithmetic with its own statistics. Germany publishes occupation medians, and Eurostat publishes the load.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">German employees in advertising and marketing earned a median 64,923 euros in 2025, full time, bonuses included; designers at specialist level 49,028 euros; public relations staff 73,562 euros.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Destatis, the German federal statistics office, earnings survey 2025, a mandatory survey of up to 58,000 establishments.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Germany's hourly labor cost was 45.0 euros in 2025, of which 34.5 euros was wages and salaries, a load of about 1.30. France's load was about 1.48, with the EU's highest non-wage share at 32.3 percent.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Eurostat, labour cost levels 2025, published March and April 2026, enterprises with 10 or more employees.</p>

  <p>At those figures a German marketing employee costs about 1,629 euros a week, a design specialist about 1,230 euros, and a public relations employee about 1,845 euros. This page applies no load to the UK figures, so they are pay before employer costs.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Full-time UK marketing associate professionals earned a median 33,412 pounds in April 2025; graphic and multimedia designers 32,510 pounds; public relations professionals 37,563 pounds.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Office for National Statistics, Annual Survey of Hours and Earnings 2025, provisional, based on a 1 percent sample of employees.</p>

  <p>That's about 643, 625 and 722 pounds a week in pay alone. In every market the in-house week is the cheapest per post only when the person is fully used, and the most expensive when they aren't. The <a href="/resources/insights/in-house-studio-vs-outsourced-production">in-house studio or outsourced production analysis</a> on this site works that utilization question through for a full creative team.</p>

  <h2>What do freelancers charge for social content?</h2>

  <p>No official series covers freelancers: the US, UK and German wage statistics count employees only. So the rates below come from published surveys and booking data, each with its sample.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">US freelance social media managers billed an average $54 an hour, graphic designers $57, motion graphics designers $64 and videographers $70, on bookings made in 2024 and 2025.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a freelance contractor marketplace's 2026 rates report, drawn from more than 182,000 of its own booking and rate data points; a market claim, since it sells the contractors it prices.</p>

  <p>The same report gives UK day rates of 326 pounds for a social media manager, 348 pounds for a graphic designer and 385 pounds for a motion graphics designer. For writing, a survey with a stated sample puts the UK day a little higher.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The median UK freelance copywriter's day rate was 440 pounds in 2026, up from 430 pounds a year earlier.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a UK copywriters' network survey, fieldwork February 2026, 338 freelancers answering the question.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">German marketing freelancers averaged 86.70 euros an hour in early 2026; a second survey put marketing and communication at 94 euros and design and media at 89 euros.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: two project marketplaces' 2026 freelancer surveys, of more than 3,300 and 1,310 freelancers; market claims.</p>

  <p>The week's freelance bill is the day rate times the days the week takes. That second number is yours. No published measure of hours per social post carries a method; the figures that circulate come from sellers estimating their own product.</p>

