  <p>Say the defined week takes two days of a social media manager and one of a designer. That's about $1,320 in the US. If it takes three days of each, it's about $2,664. Swap in your own days; the rate is the only published part. Neither figure covers the stock licenses, the tools a freelancer expects you to provide, or the hours your manager spends briefing and reviewing.</p>

  <h2>What do agencies charge for social media content?</h2>

  <p>Agencies sell a monthly tier. The ones that publish prices print a post count, a number of networks and what's excluded.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Agencies that publish their social media management prices list monthly tiers from $99 to $8,280 in the US, 99 to 2,200 pounds in the UK, and 49 to 3,000 euros in Ireland and Germany.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: 17 agencies' own price pages, read October 14, 2026, tiers as printed, no agency named; seller-published, with no method behind them.</p>

  <p>Dividing each tier by the posts it includes shows what the spread is made of.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Published tier type</th>
          <th scope="col">Price per post, rounded</th>
          <th scope="col">Defined week, 10 posts</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Volume tier, template graphics</th>
          <td>$9 to $10; 3 to 16 pounds; 2 to 10 euros</td>
          <td>$93 to $99; 30 to 153 pounds; 23 to 100 euros</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Designed posts, some video</th>
          <td>$38 to $63; 20 to 27 pounds; 19 to 167 euros</td>
          <td>$375 to $625; 205 to 267 pounds; 191 to 1,667 euros</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Full service with community management and video</th>
          <td>$345 to $540</td>
          <td>$3,450 to $5,400</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>Per-post prices are the published monthly price divided by the posts each tier includes, collected October 14, 2026. The week is ten times that.</p>

  <p>The cheapest tiers buy a template, filled in at volume. The top tiers buy someone managing the account: replies, monitoring, reporting, the video edit. Read what each tier includes before the price, because the printed terms carry costs of their own:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ad spend is excluded</strong> on the pages that mention it.</li>
    <li><strong>Setup or onboarding fees</strong> of $200 and $300 appear on two US pages.</li>
    <li><strong>Minimum terms</strong> run from none to three months and, on one German page, twelve.</li>
    <li><strong>Your own material.</strong> Some entry tiers make content only from photos and video the client supplies.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>That's why <a href="/resources/insights/real-cost-of-brand-content-2026">the real cost of brand content in 2026</a>, which prices the making of an asset, leaves social retainers out of its bands: a retainer bundles the making with the managing, and the tier doesn't say how the price splits.</p>

